# sdkconfig overlay applied ONLY to -DTR_BOARD_M2=1 builds (the Tinker-Beetle
# V2), on top of sdkconfig.defaults and sdkconfig.defaults.v9. Selected by the
# project CMakeLists, not by the build-dir name.
#
# It sets the one thing sdkconfig.defaults cannot share with this board: the
# boot flash. The V2's out computer is an ESP32-S3FH4R2, which boots from the
# 4 MB of flash in its package; the external NOR every other board carries is
# gone. The base declares 8 MB because partitions.csv ends at 6.14 MB, and on a
# 4 MB part that is the FATAL direction: the bootloader finds less flash than
# the image header claims and stops before any firmware runs, with no log of
# ours to say why (the "Boot NOR size" block in sdkconfig.defaults, and
# docs/board-versioning.md).
#
# So this board gets a table that fits, partitions_4mb.csv, and the declaration
# that covers it. Still the policy every project follows (#916): the smallest
# standard size that fits the build's own partition table, which
# tinkerrocket-idf/tools/check_flash_declarations.py checks for this build too.
# The two lines go together. Either one alone fails the build: gen_esp32part.py
# refuses partitions.csv in 4 MB, and the checker refuses 8 MB for a table that
# ends below 4 MB.
#
# It is named for what it sets and not for the board, because the
# Tinker-Mantis V11's out computer is the same part and will need the same two
# lines when it gets a board target.
#
# TRAP, the same one as everywhere else: IDF does not re-apply sdkconfig
# defaults to an sdkconfig that already exists. A build_m2 dir configured
# before this file existed keeps declaring 8 MB. main.cpp refuses to compile an
# M2 image without CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_4MB; delete build_m2/sdkconfig
# and build again when it does.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_4MB=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions_4mb.csv"
